\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Incidence and the exterior derivative}
\date{2026-11-04}


\p{The potential of the preceding discussion is a field on the vertices: one
value per site, added and scaled pointwise as in the
[vertex-field module](fdip-0010). Along an edge the difference between the two
endpoint values is what the edge carries, so the
[ring-valued incidence map](fdip-0010) takes a potential to the field strength
read off the edges. On a cubic lattice whose steps are #{x\to x+\mu},
that difference is #{\varphi(x+\mu)-\varphi(x)}.}

\p{In the forms notation the potential is a #{0}-form and the field strength a
#{1}-form, related by #{d}. The incidence map plays the role of #{d} on
#{0}-forms, and it is not #{d}: the exterior derivative raises the degree of a
form, while the incidence map runs from vertex fields to edge fields of one
particular graph. A [field pullback](fdip-0010) is composition with a map of the
carriers, as in the substitution #{f'(x)=f(h(x))}.}
